Abstract
In this work, a magnetic bio-metal-organic framework (MBMOF) nanocomposite with porous-layer open morphology is synthesized through a simple sonochemical approach and its effects on Leishmania major (MRHO/IR/75/ER) under both in-vitro and in-vivo conditions are investigated. The effects of sonication time, initial concentration of reagents and sonication power on size and morphology of MBMOF nanocomposites have been investigated and optimized. A comparison was then made between the structural information of the nanostructures and that of the bio-metal-organic framework crystals. Using the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), energy dispersive analysis of X-ray (EDAX), vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and BrunauerEmmet-Teller (BET) techniques, the prepared MBMOF nanocomposites were characterized. The mean numbers of promastigotes (cell/ml) in different MBMOF concentrations (3.12, 6.25, 12.5, 25, 50, 100, 200 and $400~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) were determined by direct counting after 24, 48 and 72 hours. Using MTT assays, the cytotoxic impacts of the MBMOF nanocomposites on promastigotes, intracellular amastigotes, and J774 macrophages were estimated. In order to investigate their therapeutic effects, the prepared MBMOF nanocomposites (25 and $12.5\upmu\mathrm{g\mL^{-1}})$ were used as ointment three times a week to treat Leishmania major in BALB/c mice. The lesion size and weight of mice were assessed before and during the treatment. The parasitic loads were measured in spleen and liver through the culture. After $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the INF- $\cdot\gamma$ and IL-4 cytokines levels in the supernatant of the spleen culture were measured. To the best of the authors’ knowledge, this study is the first to attempt to synthesize the bio-MOFs through an in-situ sonosynthesis route under ultrasound irradiation and examine their cytotoxicity effects on Leishmania major under in-vitro and in-vivo conditions.
Keywords: Fe3O4@bio-MOF nanostructures, Ultrasound irradiation, Sonosynthesis, Porous-layer open, Leishmania major.
## 1. Introduction
Leishmania parasites, which are found in the tropical and subtropical areas of the world, are responsible for the incidence of cutaneous leishmaniasis (CL) as a skin infection throughout the world and especially in the developing countries [1, 2]. The lifecycle of these parasitic protozoa is digenetic. At the beginning of their lifecycle, these parasites have flagella and experience a promastigote step as observed in the sandfly vector. Then, in the mammalian host, the parasites go through an aflagellate amastigote step. Based on the reports, twelve million people are affected by this skin infection. From 2010 to 2015, the number of people infected has amounted to thirteen million [3-5]. Erythematous papules and nodules are among the signs and symptoms of this infectious disease, along with a volcano-like central ulcer, which is likely to leave a permanent scar on the skin in spite of current prescribed medical treatments. In this context, different scholars have sought to develop effective treatment strategies with reasonable budget and viable diagnostic mechanisms. However, these diagnoses have proved to be ineffective and have also shown a high failure rate of treatment in endemic places. Moreover, said drugs have low sensitivity and are expensive, nonspecific, and difficult to utilize in field conditions [6-8].
In view of this, eradication of the leishmaniasis endemics is very hard and the disease reflux may cause a serious problem if effective treatment is not found. In the last decade, due to the complex lifecycle of leishmania species and expensiveness of the pharmaceutical process for the production of the related drugs, scant attention has been paid to the preparation of more appropriate drugs to treat leishmaniasis. As reported by the World Health Organization (WHO), the selective systemic and intra lesional treatments against CL involve antimony based drugs, sodium stibogluconate (Pentostam), and meglumine antimoniate (Glucantime). In this context, an urgent need can be felt to find safe, effective treatments against leishmaniasis. In recent years, impressive novel compounds have been developed for CL treatment which are topically applied to lesions and may inhibit the drug resistance [9-12]. However, the antimony based drugs for cutaneous leishmaniasis treatment make use of an invasive method with their intradermal injection. Again, it is of great importance to develop a non-invasive and more efficient method, which has not yet been developed.
Due to their therapeutic impacts as antitumor, antimicrobial agents and drug receptors, the medical application of the nanoparticles (NPs) has recently attracted much attention among researchers [13-15]. A number of research groups have already studied the impacts of some nanoparticles upon the Leishmania Major [16,17]. Since NPs take the advantages of having large surface areas, fine size, regular morphology and electrical charge, they show an increased efficiency in biomedicine [18]. Among the nanoparticles with such distinctive properties are the magnetic ferrite nanoparticles that demonstrate magnetic features and have been employed in various fields of study, but  particularly against the pathogens in planktonic fauna [19].  Increasing attention has been paid to the superparamagnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles which are nontoxic and play an important part in the biomedical applications. These applications involve localized hyperthermia therapy, targeted delivery, labeling and tracking of the stem cells, and contrast agency of the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) technique. The latter, i.e., the preparation of some novel, more efficient MRI contrast agents has shown to be a matter of great importance and has extensively been studied by researchers. In this regard, it was realized that, as $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are biocompatible and hydrophilic [20, 21], which drives applications in the domains of cell separation, adsorption of heavy metals, protein separation and biosensors [22-25]. Through their surface modification or functionalization with amino acids, peptides and biomolecules, they $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles having these characteristics are characterized. Since $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles’ surficial area can straightforwardly be modified, it will be possible to conjugate the targeting moieties with the surface of the particles in order to perform the site-specific targeting [26, 27]. In our work, a straightforward protocol for the synergic combination of magnetic particles with the metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) has been provided.
Through the organic bridging ligands, the metal ions or clusters are linked together to form MOFs, which are a group of crystallized porous polymeric substances. Different applications such as separation, exchange, and storage of gas, drug delivery, heterogeneous catalysis, and biomedical applications have been considered for MOFs due to their permanent and large volume porosity, high specific surface area, tunable pore size, and outer surface modification. Since MOFs show different functional groups in their frameworks, they have a better chemical manageability than other porous materials such as zeolites, mesoporous silica and active carbon [28-30]. Different MOFs are usually synthesized based on the intended usage. For example, Rosi et al. prepared the bio-MOFs, metal-adeninate organic frameworks with high chemical stability, in which adenine was utilized as a basic bio-molecule building block [31, 32]. Moreover, since adenine is rigid and has numerous metal binding sites, it can be used as an ideal bio-molecular ligand to produce the bio-MOFs [33].
In comparison with the pure MOFs, in case that MOFs are combined with functional species or matrix materials, not only will the properties be improved, but also novel applications for MOFs will be provided. These new applications (e.g., bio-imaging and drug delivery) are obtained because the functional species/matrix can interact with the structures of the MOF. As an instance, in order to investigate and treat the tumor cells, it is usually required to take account of the biomedical applications of the porous materials in combination with the magnetic nanoparticles [34-36] Consequently, it can be proposed that, when bioactivity is combined with imaging in MOFs, an interesting opportunity is provided for the application of the MOF-based theranostics-following biomedicine in the human body via the MOFs imaging characteristics.
Numerous preparation routs including diffusion, hydrothermal, and solvothermal approaches have already been introduced to synthesize metal-organic frameworks. However, said approaches mostly require long reaction times, along with high pressures and temperatures. Furthermore, since MOFs demonstrate considerable potential for industrial uses, great importance might be attached to the provision of costefficient synthesis routes using technologically-new methods. Recently, sonochemistry has been used for the synthesis of nanomaterial. Compared to conventional synthetic approaches, where some reactions failed to take place entirely, ultrasound can often facilitate said reactions, even at room temperature. Compared to the thermal energy sources, ultrasonic irradiation is ecofriendly and gives high yields with a short reaction time. Furthermore, ultrasound is considerably inexpensive, straightforward, and controllable, while using a green route for the coordination of supramolecular compounds on a nano scale [37, 38]. Due to the highly intense ultrasonic illumination of the liquid, acoustic cavitation was formed in the medium by the shock waves. The cavitation caused by intense ultrasound (20 kHz–10 MHz) lead to the hot spots formation inside the bubbles under a pressure of 1000 bar and a temperature of 5000 K with the heating and cooling rate $>$ $10^{10}~\mathrm{~K~}\mathrm{~s~}^{-1}$ which are unattainable in normal condition, and accordingly the ultrasonic energy has increasingly been in the focus of scholarly interest [39-42]. In recent years, ultrasound has been generally used to prepare porous materials. In one hour or more, MOFs might be synthesized in aqueous solution using ultrasound. In this study, a green and straightforward synthetic method has been developed for the preparation of a nanostructured porous bio-MOF $\mathrm{(Zn_{8}(A d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2(N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2})^{+}}$ , 8DMF, $11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}),$ ). To this end, through ultrasound at room temperature and under atmospheric pressure, zinc(II) acetate dihydrate, adenine and 4,4'-biphenyl dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) have been reacted.
As far as we know, no previous studies have been conducted to investigate the application of the magnetic bio-MOF for curing the cutaneous leishmaniasis. Firstly, bio-MOFs with uniform porous-layer open morphology are obtained by optimizing  sonication time, initial concentration of reagents and ultrasonic irradiation frequency. After that, using  in vitro conditions, the impacts of the prepared magnetic bio-MOFs on the development of the promastigote and amastigote stages of Leishmania Major were investigated and evaluated. Finally, the impacts of the magnetic bio-MOF on the treatment of the cutaneous leishmaniasis in the murine model in BALB/c mice was assessed.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Material
The following chemicals were used in the synthesis procedure: adenine (vitamin ${\bf B}_{4}$ $99\%$ , Aldrich), iron(III) chloride tetrahydrate $\mathrm{\FeCl}_{3}$ . $\mathrm{4H_{2}O}$ $99\%$ , Merck), Mercaptoacetic acid (MAA $98\%$ , Merck), zinc acetate dihydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}$ . $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $99\%$ , Merck) as precursor salts; nitric acid $(\mathrm{HNO_{3}}65\%$ , Merck) an a strong oxidizing agent; ammonia solution $(\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}\mathrm{~\}25\%$ , Merck) as a reducing agent; dioctyl sulfosuccinate sodium salt (aerosol–OT, AOT $98\%$ , Aldrich) as a surfactant; cyclohexane $(\geq99.9\%$ , Merck) as the oil phase; ethanol $(\geq99.9\%$ , Merck) and N,N-Dimethyllformamide (DMF $99\%$ , Merck) as washing agents and solvents; and the water used in the experiments was deionized (DI) and doubly distilled prior to it’s use as the aqueous phase in emulsion system and solvent. The ligand of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid (4,4´-BPDC) was prepared by the reported methods [43]. 3-(4,5-Dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyl tetrazolium bromide (MTT) and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were purchased from Amresco (USA). All other chemicals were analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Characterization
In the present study, field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE–SEM), energy-dispersive X–ray spectroscopy (EDS), powder X–ray diffraction (PXRD), thermo-gravimetric analyses (TGA), vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM), Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET), and Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT–IR) were used to characterize the surficial morphology, size, elemental composition, and crystalline structure of the magnetic bio-metal–organic framework. In order to obtain the FE-SEM images, a Hitachi S1460 field emission scanning electron microscope, which had an accelerating voltage of $15~\mathrm{kV}$ , was used. The EDS spectrum was obtained on a JEOL JSM–6380LV scanning electron microscope and the  PXRD analyses were carried out for all the samples on a Philips diffractometer device (Model TM–1800). X–ray’s, $(\lambda=1.542\mathrm{~\AA})$ ), were produced by a nickel filtered Cu–Kα radiation source. A proportional counter detector was used at $4^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ scan rate and a  current of $30~\mathrm{mA}$ was employed and the scanning angle was in the range of $1^{\circ}$ to $80^{\circ}$ while the used voltage was 40-kV. FT–IR spectra with wavenumbers of $400{\cdot}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were obtained through KBr disks run on a Shimadzu FT–IR model Prestige 21 spectrometer. The absorptiondesorption isotherms were estimated via a Nova Station A instrument at $77\mathrm{~K~N}_{2}$ . Besides, specific surface areas were calculated using the Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) approach. In order to estimate the pore-size distributions, the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) technique was used and also the isotherm desorption branch was taken into account. In order to calculate magnetic characteristics, a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) (BHV-55, Riken, Japan) at the room temperature was utilized. Using a thermo-balance model of TG209 F1 Iris, the thermo-gravimetric analyses (TGA) were performed. Through the nitrogen gas flow of $30~\mathrm{{mL}\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ , at a rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ , the prepared samples were then heated. A Philips device, model EM208S, with the accelerating voltage of $100\mathrm{kV}$ was used to conduct TEM measurements of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOF nanostructures.
## 2.3. Synthesizing the functionalized $F e_{3}O_{4}$ nanoparticles
Synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles was performed in accordance with one of our previous studies [44]. In brief, mixing 3 emulsions for 2 hours at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the nanoparticles were produced, which were subsequently washed and dried uo so as to be used in the next synthesis stage. For preparation of the mercapto-acetic acid (MAA)-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, the following process was used [45]. First, under shaking for 24 hours, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(0.5\ \mathrm{g})$ was added to a solution of mercapto-acetic acid in ethanol ( $100~\mathrm{mL}$ , $2.9\ \mathrm{mM}$ ). Then, through the application of an external magnetic field, the product was collected and finally washed with water.
2.4. Synthesis of $Y n_{8}(A d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2(N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2})^{+}$ , 8DMF, 11H2O as single crystal
According to the reported method [46], rod-shaped colorless crystals of Bio-MOFs were obtained from a mixture of zinc acetate dihydrate $\mathrm{(0.375~mmol)}$ ), adenine (0.125 mmol), 4,4'-biphenyl dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) $\mathrm{0.25~mmol})$ , DMF ( $\begin{array}{r}{{13.5}\mathrm{\mL},}\end{array}$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{i}\ \mathrm{mL}$ ) and nitric acid (1 mmol). The final reaction mixture was added to a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vial and heated at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , then cooled at $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ h to room temperature. Finally, rod-shaped colorless crystals were collected and washed with DMF $(3\mathrm{~x~}3\mathrm{~mL}$ ), and dried under Ar gas (30 min) (yield $0.79\mathrm{g}$ , $82\%$ based on adenine).
2.5. Synthesis of $Z n_{8}(A d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2(N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2})^{+}$ , 8DMF, $I I H_{2}O$ nano-structures by a sonochemical process
To prepare $\mathrm{Zn_{8}(A d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2(N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2})^{+}}$ , 8DMF, $11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nano-structures, zinc acetate dihydrate, adenine and BPDC with molar ratio 1:1:1 were added to a round bottomed flask containing DMF $(20~\mathrm{mL})$ and treated by ultrasound irradiation in ultrasonic bath. The obtained precipitates were filtered off, washed with DMF several times and then dried in air.
## 2.6. MAA-functionalized Fe3O4@bio-MOFs synthesis
To prepare the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs nanocomposite, a suspension of MAA-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles $(0.2\ {\tt g})$ in DMF $\mathrm{20~ml}$ was prepared under ultrasound irritation and added to a mixture of zinc acetate dihydrate, adenine and BPDC with molar ratio 1:1:1 in  DMF $20~\mathrm{mL})$ ). Then the mixture was transferred into the ultrasonic bath in ambient temperature and atmospheric pressure. After 30 mins, under ultrasound irradiation $(40\mathrm{kHz})$ , using an external magnetic field, the prepared functionalized MBMOFs were subsequently gathered from the reaction mixture. Using DMF and DI water, the functionalized MBMOFs were washed a number of times, and finally they were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in vacuo.
## 2.7. In vitro assay
## 2.7.1. Culturing of parasites and preparation of nanoparticles
$L.$ major parasites (MRHO/IR/75/ER) were obtained from the Razi Vaccine and Serum Research Institute of Iran. In order to increase the number of parasites, a promastigotes culture was carried out in RPMI 1640 medium, and accordingly penicillin (100 unit $\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ ), streptomycin ( $100\ \upmu\mathrm{g\ml^{-1}}.$ ) as well as FBS $20\%$ v/v) were used in a $25\pm1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ incubator. Dissolution of  bio-MOF (1mg) in $50\%$ ethanol and $50\%$ distilled water was carried out so that the functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ @bio-MOFs composite stock dissolution could be obtained.
## 2.7.2. Promastigote assay
In order to investigate the anti-leishmanial effects of the functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite upon the promastigotes of L. major parasites, at the presence of various concentrations (400, 200 , 100, 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25 and $3.12~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1},$ ) of functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite, the early log phase promastigotes of $L.$ major were developed in an RPMI culture medium, which was enriched with $20\%$ (v/v) FBS in 96-well plates in initiation concentration of $2\times{10}^{6}$ cells $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{l}^{-1}$ as triplicate. For reproduction, the samples were kept at a temperature of $24~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For detection of the anti-promastigote behavior, the promastigotes in the hemocytometer chamber (Neubauer chamber) were directly counted after 24 hours, 48 hours, and 72 hours of incubation, and then the results compared with those of the control group. With reference to the control plates, the promastigotes were cultured as triplicate in the absence of the functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite. For determination of the $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ , GraphPad prism 5.1 was employed.
2.7.3. Toxic effects of functionalized Fe3O4@bio-MOFs composite on L. major promastigotes
MTT assay was taken into account to obtain promastigotes viability. In brief, in 96-well plates, in the presence of 8 concentrations of functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite (3.12,6.25, 12.5, 25, 50, 100, 200 and $400\ \upmu\mathrm{g\ml^{-1}},$ ), the addition of promastigotes $(100~\upmu\mathrm{l})$ having $2{\times}10^{6}$ cells $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{l}^{-1}$ to $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ RPMI medium was considered. The plate was then incubated in a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ incubator ( $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $95\%$ damp) at a temperature of $24~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 72 hours. Subsequently, into each well, a solution of MTT [3-(4, 5-methylthiazol2-yl)-2, 5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide] $(20~\upmu\mathrm{l})$ was added. The solution was then incubated once more for 4 hours. Concerning the control group, RPMI $1640\ (200\ \upmu\mathrm{l\ml^{-1}}$ ) was added to the wells, which contained only promastigotes. In order to prevent the vaporization of the well contents, PBS $(200~\upmu\mathrm{l})$ was added around the well plates. After the passing of 4 hours, centrifuge of the plates at ${3,000\mathrm{g}}$ was taken into account for 15 minutes. Afterwards, in order to distinguish the yellow tetrazolium crystals from the insoluble blue formazan color, of dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) $(100~\upmu\mathrm{l})$ was added to each well. This could not be accomplished by the perished or decayed cells. Lastly, the optical density (OD) of the plates was read in ELISA reader at $570\ \mathrm{nm}$ . In order to measure the percentage of viability, the following formula was adopted: $100\times$ (absorbance of treated cells/absorbance of control cells) $\times100$ .
2.7.4. Toxic effects of functionalized Fe3O4@bio-MOFs composite upon the uninfected macrophage cells In the present work, J774 macrophages, which were stored and frozen at a temperature of $-70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , were obtained from Department of Medical Parasitology in Tarbiat Modarres University of Tehran, Iran. First, the J774 macrophage cells were cultured in a RPMI 1640 with FBS $10\%$ (v/v), penicillin (100 unit $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{l}^{-1}$ ), and streptomycin ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}.$ ),. Next, at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and in a $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , these macrophage cells were incubated. The uninfected macrophage cells assay was conducted in a 96-well plate. The cultured macrophage cells $(\mathrm{100\upmuL},\mathrm{1\times10^{5}c e l l m l^{-1}}.$ ) were added to the wells. Using a fresh medium of culture, the unstuck cells were deleted. With regard to the adhered cells, the culture medium was then replaced and remodeled with RPMI culture medium $(100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , in which various volumes of the functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite (3.13, 6.25, 12.5, 25, 50, 100, 200 and $400~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1},$ were used. Using the MTT assay, the macrophages viability was estimated after 72 hours from the start of the culture.
## 2.7.5. Amastigote assay
Initially, on a glass coverslip, the J774 macrophage cells $(1\times10^{5}\mathrm{cell\ml^{-1}},$ ) were embedded into the tissue culture of 12-well plates, and subsequently they were incubated at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for 24 hours. Unadhered cells were then removed. Via the stationary phase of promastigotes, the adhered macrophages were infected at a ratio of 10:1 (parasite/macrophage). After that, the plate was incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $5\%$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ for 24 hours. Through the application of the cold phosphate buffer salin (PBS), the additional promastigotes were washed and omitted. Incubation of the infected macrophage cells were carried out for 72 hours in the presence of different functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite concentrations (6.25, 12.5, 25 and $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ $\mathbf{m}^{\mathsf{r}^{\mathsf{I}^{\star}}}$ ). Under a light microscope with Giemsa stain, the intra amastigotes of 100 macrophages on a coverslip were counted. The results were subsequently compared with the untreated control group.
## 2.8. In vivo assay
## 2.8.1. Animals
From the Laboratory Animals Unit of Pasteur Institute of Iran, 30 female BALB/C mice with the mean weight of 16 to 18 grams were purchased. These mice, which were all aged 5 to 7 weeks, were moved to the Animal House of Tarbiat Modarres University of Tehran and kept under standard conditions of temperature $(22\pm1{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in a $12\mathrm{h}$ day/night cycle. The mice were fed with a standard pellet diet while they were provided with water ad libitum. Approval of the animal treaty was issued by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of Tarbiat Modares University of Medical Sciences’ Ethical Laws Committee. Subcutaneous injection  of $L.$ major promastigotes $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{\cdot}1\ m L}$ , $2\times{10}^{6}$ cells mL-1) – collected from the stationary phase – into the base of the mice tails was carried out so that the female BALB/c mice could be infected with L. major. The said thirty mice were then categorized into 6 groups in a way that 5 mice were involved in each group. For group 1, a dose of $25~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ of MOF solution was applied. Group 2 was given a dose of IC50 $(12.5\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ of MOF solution, which was provided through the in vitro conditions. Glucantim (20 mg/kg/day) intraperitoneal was given to the positive control group1, whereas the control group 2 was supplied with Vaseline. No treatment was considered for the control group 3. With regard to control group 4, ethanol was employed as MOF solution. For 21 days, these mice were topically treated three times weekly. Before the beginning of the treatment, using a caliper the lesions sizes were measured. Furthermore, the body weight of each of the mice under study was monitored.
## 2.8.2. Preparation of Ointment
Having a Vaseline base, the functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOFs composite ointment was synthesized with the concentrations of $25~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ and $12.5~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ . MOF $(250~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ was mixed in $10\ \mathrm{ml}$ of Vaseline. After the ointment preparation, $0.1~\mathrm{ml}$ of it was topically applied to the lesions 3 times per week. From the $25~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ ointment concentration, $12.5~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ concentration was produced.
## 2.8.3. Cytokine assay
After 21 consecutive days, when the treatment was finished, the $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ and IL-4 levels were calculated. 2 mice from each of the considered groups were killed, in  order to extract the spleen lymphocytes. In 24-well plates in the RPMI1640 cell culture medium (GibCo, USA) which involved $10\%$ (v/v) fetal bovine serum (FBS; GibCo, USA) and 100 unit $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{l}^{-1}$ penicillin, streptomycin $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ (Sigma, Germany), $2\times10^{6}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ lymphocytes were grown. The culture of the lymphocyte cells was initiated using the soluble Leishmania antigens (SLA) having a concentration of $20~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ which was produced after the L. Major promastigotes were again suspended in sterile PBS at a concentration of around $10^{8}\mathrm{\cellsmL^{-1}}$ . Through 6 freeze-thaw cycles, the promastigotes of the stationary phase were lysed. After that, these promastigotes were centrifugalized at a temperature of $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for a 12 minutes. The supernatant substances were collected, and then using the Bradford assay, the concentration of the proteins was calculated. SLA $(20~\mathrm{\upmug/ml})$ , was added to the wells,then the plates were subsequently incubated in $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 72 hours. The supernatants were then collected and  reserved at $-80~^{\circ}C$ to be further utilized. Using the enzyme-linked immune-sorbent assay (ELISA) kit (R & D) in compliance with the instructions of its manufacturer, measurement of the cytokine levels was performed.
2.8.4. Estimation of the weight and the size of lesions in BALB/c mice
Using a digital scale and a vernier caliper, the lesion sizes and body weights of the mice were calculated every six days. The measurements were performed before (week 0) and during (weeks 1, 2, and 3) the treatment.
## 2.8.5. Evaluation of parasite burden
For evaluation of parsite burden, we cultured $30~\mathrm{mg}$ of crashed spleen and liver tissue in RPMI1640 medium plus $10\%$ FBS. After one week the number of parasites was calculated using a Neubar chamber according to modified method of Witney et al [47].
## 2.8.6. Statistical analysis
Analyses of the in vitro experiments, body weights of the mice, lesion size, as well as the cytokine assay were conducted by nonparametric independent groups through the Anova one way and Mann-Whitney assay. Data analysis was performed using the SPSS software version 21 and IC50 measured Graph Pad Prism. In order to extract the graphs, Excel software was employed. In this work, the statistical differences was presumed to be $P<0.05$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
Based on the single crystal structural investigation, any singular cage contains four adeninates occupying alternating faces of an octahedron. Each cage involves eight $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ tetrahedra, two at apical positions and four on the corners of an equatorial plane. Half of the $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ clusters are made of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ centers that involve apexes and connects neighboring octahedral cages together. Two adeninates are bridged via the N3 and N9 positions are used to bridged $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ pairs in each $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ cluster. For any adeninate, N1 and N7 are in coordination with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in equatorial plane. Multiple BPDC linkers are used for interconnecting the columns of zinc-adeninate. In $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}}}$ cluster, we have mono-dentate coordination of one carboxylate from each linker to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and mono-dentate binding of the other carboxylate to an equatorial $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ on one neighboring column (Figs. 1-3). Due to such a pattern of connectivity, some large channels are resulted which run through the c crystallographic direction. Based on network topology of pcu, parallel packing of the zinc-adeninate columns is seen. [48].
