Using the hydrothermal technique, rod-shaped, colorless single crystals were obtained from the combination of $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}.H_{2}O}$ , adenine, and 4,4'-BPDC ligands were solvothermally synthesized after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ using DMF solvents which are suitable for X-ray. Nevertheless, using ultrasound and with the same solvents, bio-MOF micro/nanostructures were synthesized at room temperature just in some minutes or hours. Fig. 4 gives a summary of various synthesis methods concerning $\mathrm{Zn_{8}(A d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2(N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2})^{+}},$ , 8DMF, 11H2O.
To investigate the nanostructured $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOF, we changed the sonication time parameter at fixed pressure $(0.04{\bf M})$ and power (45 W) of bio-MOF initial concentration. In shorter times, distribution of the structures and their morphology were highly various. Different sizes and further agglomeration in the morphology of these compounds were observed in the shorter times. Moreover, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in shorter sonication times were completely agglomerated in the frameworks. With the increase in the time of ultrasonic irradiation, in spite of the increase in the size of  the bio-MOF structure, a non-agglomerated porous-layer open morphology was resulted. Moreover, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were well distributed in MOF. Hence, sonication time of 120 min was selected (Fig. 5).
Subsequently, the bio-MOF concentration was increased two times as great as its initial concentration (0.08 M) and then it was reduced to half of its initial concentration $(0.02{\ }\mathrm{M})$ . As shown in the FE-SEM images of Fig. 6a and b, as the original bio-MOF concentration $(0.04\ \mathbf{M})$ was reduced $(0.02{\textbf{M}})$ , some porous layer open morphologies with uniformity were attained. Although the sonication time in this case was similar to that of the previous case (in the presence of the 0.04M of MOF precursors concentration), no changes were observed in the morphology and agglomeration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles. However, due to the lower consumption of the precursor concentration and the cost-efficiency of using low concentrations, the precursor concentration of 0.02M was selected as optimal sample. Also, As shown in Fig. 6b, with twice increasing of the precursors concentration, a slight reduction occurred in the uniformity of hole open structures and agglomeration increased.
On the other hand, we employed two sonication powers of 30 and 60 W to investigate the sonication power impact upon the size and morphology of the chosen sample (120 min, 0.02 M). According to FE-SEM patterns in Fig. 7a and b, with the increase in the power of irradiation, the morphological non-uniformity and porous-layer open agglomeration were removed and nanoparticles were mostly in the surface of bioMOF, which can easily be eliminated in the later stages from the structure and can change the magnetic effects. Furthermore, using the higher ultrasound power (60 W), although the porous-layer open structure of the compound was formed, a different morphology also appeared which resulted in a reduction in the uniformity of the morphology and the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were highly agglomerated.
Generally, as the temperature rose to $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the solubility increased, which lead to the reduction in the growth species super-saturation in liquid medium [49]. In between, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are mono-dispersed in the form of a quite long tread with a mean diameter of $35~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . In MBMOFs, two separate phases of bioMOFs and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are characterized. Also, using TEM analysis, the morphology of the MBMOF nanocomposites were further characterized (Fig. 8). Results of the TEM analysis also matched with those of the FE-SEM analysis.
Also, through FE-SEM analyses, as shown in Fig. 9, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and pure bio-MOFs synthesized via ultrasound sonication were morphologically characterized. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are spherical in shape, have a mean particle size of $35~\mathrm{nm}$ , and are homogenously dispersed. In the present study, in accordance with the recorded literature [50], through the emulsion nanoreactors, the composition and size of our synthesized nanostructures have been controlled. Bio-MOFs on the other hand are observed to be porouslayer open, and their surface area is smooth, each layer having athickness of $30{-}60~\mathrm{nm}$ (which is associated with the bio-MOF framework).
In the present study, as shown in Fig. 10, the XRD technique was used to characterize the phase structure and crystallinity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, bio-MOFs, and MBMOF nanocomposites. As Fig. 10a shows, the diffraction peaks in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are all properly indexed to the face centered cubic structure: $a=$ 8.361(02) Å and $\mathrm{V}(a^{3})=69.906655(4404)$ $(\mathring\mathbf{A}^{3})$ are observed to be the lattice parameters, which are in compliance with JCPDS card (JCPDS file 19-0629). No diffraction peaks were seen for the other impurity phases, and therefore it can be inferred that our prepared product is highly pure. Moreover, the high crystallinity of the synthesized product can be understood from the fact that the observed diffraction peaks are intense. Figs. 10b and c demonstrates that the peaks in accord with the literature structure for the pure bio-MOF [31, 51]. Obtained XRD pattern of single crystal X-ray data of bio-MOF is compared with the experimental XRD of as synthesized nano-structure of bio-MOF through sonochemical process. According to our observations, a good agreement exists between these XRD patterns. Therefore, it can be inferred that the sonochemically-synthesized nanostructured compound and the compound resulting from single crystal diffraction are equal. Since the peaks are significantly broadened,  it can be understood that these particles are nano-dimensioned. On the other hand, from Fig. 10d, it can be observed that after $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were added onto the framework of the bio-MOFs, the diffraction peaks of XRD did not change, which indicates that application of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles did not exert any obvious effects upon the structure of the bio-MOF framework. It was also revealed that the synthesized MBMOF nanocomposites were appropriately crystalline. Besides, no change occurred in the porosity of functionalized bio-MOFs. In this view, since the bio-MOF crystal frameworks have enough stability, they can be applied and extended to new fields.
As shown in Fig. 11, FT-IR spectroscopy was used to investigate the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in the bio-MOF frameworks. FT-IR spectroscopy of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in Fig. 11a showed a peak at $570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be caused by the vibrations of the Fe-O bonds. For both the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and MBMOF nanocomposites, a Fe-O vibration will be present, however since the bio-MOFs coating results in an immobility, a reduction was observed in the intensity of the said peak. The zoom bands ranging from 1350 to $1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are quite similar for the bio-MOF and MBMOF nanocomposites. The stretching vibrations of benzene and carboxylate resulted in the absorption bands at 1540 and $1380\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. Due to the NH and O-H in-plane bending vibrations, two peaks at $1430\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $933~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed. Owing to the surface-adsorbed water and hydroxyl groups, a peak was seen at $3434~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ [52-55]. The $1539~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band was caused by $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ stretching vibrations in the acid aromatic ring. At 3197 and $3344~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , the observed peak intensities can be explained by the asymmetric and symmetric stretching of the primary amines. In other words, vibrations of the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching signals at 3344 and $3197~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ show that in the bio-MOF framework, a free amine function is carried out by adenine, and so in hetero-cycle, zinc is connected to the atoms of nitrogen. As a result, as $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were used in the transformation of bio-MOFs, no change occurs in the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretching signals, which further shows adenines presence and lack of bond to the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, instead having a free $-\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group [56-63]. By combination of the XRD and FT-IR techniques, a better understanding of how the bio-MOF nanocomposites are synthesized is provided.
Fig. 11. FTIR spectra of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles (a), bio-MOF (single crystal) (b), bio-MOF nano-structures (c), and MBMOF nanocomposites (d).
TGA analysis offers valuable information about the thermal stability of different substances. Fig. 12 shows the TGA curves for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and MBMOF nanocomposites. As seen from the thermogravimetric curve, the mass loss of the iron oxide nanoparticle before $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is equal to $4.6\%$ . Before the temperature rises to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , another $3.7\%$ of its mass is lost. Between $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the mass is slowly reduced to $2.5\%$ , which is due to the moisture loss. In case of MBMOF nanocomposites (Fig. 12b), three different regions can be seen in the TGA curve of the MBMOF, as follows: (1) between 30 and $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , a mass loss region $(15.7\%)$ showing the moisture loss is observed; (2) between 80 and $317^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , a slow mass loss region $(12\%)$ is observed which is associated with water loss from the MBMOFs combined with $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}^{+2}$ and FeO oxidation; (3) at $317^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , MBMOF structure is destroyed and the organic linker is also buried. At $323^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , mass loss is ended. Since the building blocks of zinc-adeninate are rigid and connected to other adjacent units via a number of 3 rigid BPDC struts, it can be assumed that the structure of the bio-MOFs frameworks is robust and can resist the loss of the guest molecules as solvents. Subsequently, the mass reaches stability up to a temperature of $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Out of the original mass, the stable mass  makes up $42.9\%$ . The remaining mass percentage after $323^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is made of $\mathrm{znO}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ .
EDS was employed to demonstrate the existence of metal and non-metal elements. MBMOF chemical structure is shown in Fig. 13, which is in agreement with XRD and FT-IR results. The observed peaks in this figure. are associated with Zn, Fe, C, N, and O and consequently it can be corroborated that magnetic bio-MOFs are present in the prepared nanocomposites. Au is responsible for other existing peaks in the said spectra, which is just used as the sputter coating for the EDS sample. Therefore, the present study has prepared MBMOFs with favorable properties such as good crystallinity, phase pureness, and monodispersion.
Through an experimental measurement of ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at 77K, bio-MOF and MBMOF porosity were characterized. These adsorbents are micro-porous in nature and have isotherms of Type I. In our synthesized MBMOF nanocomposites, the BET surface area was smaller than that of pure bio-MOF. In other words, while pure bio-MOF had a surface area of $1356\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , MBMOF nanocomposites show a surficial area of $1096~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . This difference is mostly because $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles do not have porosity. The average pore size of MBMOF nanocomposites is approximately 13–14 $\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ , whereas the average pore size of pure bio-MOF is larger and between 16 to $17\mathring\mathrm{A}$ . Accordingly, addition of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles has resulted in a reduction in the sizes of MBMOF micro-pores. When $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were integrated into bio-MOF pores, the pores in the framework were partially blocked. This is also the case with the other MOF composites such as MOF-Derived Cobalt-Doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}\ @\mathrm{{C}}$ [64].
Fig. 14a and b demonstrate the magnetization curves of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and MBMOFs nanocomposites. For $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, the saturation magnetization is observed to be $78.93\mathrm{\emu/g}$ at 8000 Oe (Fig. 14a), which is due to the disorder of the atomic magnetic moments or the spin glass effect on the surface of the nanoparticles [65, 66]. With regard to the bulk value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , this value is assumed to be 98 emu/g [67]. MBMOF magnetic characteristics are represented in Fig. 14b. According to this Fig., the saturated magnetization of MBMOF at 8000 Oe is equal to $36.43~\mathrm{emu/g}$ . In brief, MBMOF magnetization is observed to be lower than that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, which can be explained in terms of the fact that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ surfaces are covered by MBMOFs layers which are not magnetically active. As seen in the inset of Fig. 14, using an external magnetic field for MBMOFs magnetization, particles were properly separated. Using external magnetism, the particles were attracted to the vial wall in 20 seconds, which shows that MBMOF nanocomposites can be considered in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and biological studies. As magnetic field was applied, the mixture became transparent and homogenous redistribution of the adsorbent in the aqueous phase took place.
