3.3. Toxic effects of MAA-functionalized Fe3O4@bio-MOFs on macrophages
Using MTT assay, MBMOF toxicity effects on uninfected J774 macrophages were explored. In this context, the effects of 8 different concentrations of MBMOF after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ were measured. The concentrations were as follows: 400, 200, 100, 50, 25,12.5, 6.25 and $3.12~\mathrm{\upmug/ml}$ . As Fig. 17 shows, MBMOF nanocomposite viability on macrophage cells was found to be based on a dose-dependent response. The obtained results were then compared to untreated control group. The maximum toxicity was related to $400~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{\mL}^{-1}$ . The results show that there are marked differences between control group and MBMOF concentrations of 12.5, 25, 50, 100, 200 and $400\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ after 72 hours $(\mathrm{P}{<}0.05)$ .
## 3.4. Anti-amastigote assay
The anti-amastigote effect of the magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites on infected macrophage cells was assessed. It was found that as concentration of MBMOF nanocomposites increased, a reduction happened in intracellular amastigotes in infected macrophage cells. Compared to the control group, a remarkable decrease occurred in the average number of amastigotes in the test groups $(P{<}0.05)$ . The average number of amastigotes/macrophage after 72 h in the negative control group was 5.5. On the other hand, the average numbers of amastigotes/macrophage were 2.8, 1.9, 1.4 and 0.95 after being treated with 6.25, 25, 50, and $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}}$ of bio-MOFs, respectively (Table 1).
