For investigation of the anti-leishmanial viability of the magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites, two magnetic bio-MOF doses were applied in ointment form (25 and $12.5~\mathrm{\upmug/ml})$ to some BalB/c mice, which had been infected subcutaneously with $L.$ Major promastigotes (Fig. 18). The prepared bio-MOFs showed toxic impacts upon macrophage cells under in vitro conditions, and therefore only ointment dose was utilized under in vivo conditions. Lesions emerged after the mice were injected for three weeks. Measurement of the weights and the sizes of the lesions were accomplished before and after treatment each week. The results were then compared with the results of the control group. It was observed that Vaseline ointment with bioMOFs exerted an antileishmanial impact and prevented further progression, though by the second week an increase was observed in the sizes of the lesions. The lesion sizes in the test groups, which were subject to the bio-MOF nanocomposites, were smaller than those in the control groups $(\mathsf{p}{<}0.05)$ . As represented in Table 2, no significant difference existed in the average lesion diameter between the second group and the third one (i.e., the positive control group) $(\mathfrak{p}>0.05)$ .
