## 3.6. Cytokine assay
INF- $\cdot\gamma$ and IL-4 cytokine levels were analyzed in the test and controls using ELISA after 72 hours of exposure. It was found that when magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites were used, a considerable increase in INF- $\cdot\gamma$ and a slight increase in IL-4 cytokine levels were observed in the mice. As shown in Fig. 20, significant differences were observed in comparison with control groups $(\mathrm{P}{<}0.05)$ .
Considerable differences were observed in the averages of $\mathrm{IFN}{-}\gamma$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=0.002},$ ), IL-4 $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{P=0.004})$ ), parasite burden in the spleen $(\mathbf{P}{<}0.001)$ , and in the liver $(\mathbf{P}{<}0.001)$ in the target groups. Using Kruskal-Wallis test, the results were examined.
In this work, for the first time, the viability of magnetic bio-MOFs against the Iranian strain of  L. Major has been investigated. This study has considered the in vivo and in vitro viability of MBMOF versus the promastigotes and amastigotes of Leishmania Major. Using MTT assays, it has been shown that antileishmanial behaviors of the magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites are both dose and time-dependent. Parasite numbers were observed to be considerably different in test and control groups. As the results showed, the prepared nanocomposites influenced the amastigote and promastigote stages of L. Major; however, using similar doses of the magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites, amastigotes were observed to be more sensitive than promastigotes. As reported in the literature, such a difference can be ascribed to the morphological, structural, and biochemical variations in both parasites and nanostructure [7, 68, 69]. Similar anti-leishmanial behaviors towards $L.$ . Major and L. Infantum have already been reported for such nanomaterials as nanosilver, nanogold, nanozinc and nanoselenium. In the present study, the toxic impact of our prepared nanocomposites on uninfected macrophage cells has also been taken into account.
Under in vivo conditions, using a dose of $25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of the magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites, some considerable suppression effects were observed on the CL lesions of BALB/c mice which were affected by L. Major. On the other hand, when the mice were treated with a dose of $12.5~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ magnetic bio-MOFs, the suppression effects were moderate. Consequently, when the mice were treated with an ointment form of these nanocamposites with IC50 concentration, sizes of the lesions were reduced. In the context of the leishmaniasis in mice model, different cytokines (e.g., interferon and tumor necrosis factor) are produced by the immune system cells, and accordingly macrophage cells are activated and Th1 response is generated. Macrophage cells serve a crucial role in generation of numerous immune responses and are crucial in combatting leishmaniasis.
The said mice were given a dose with $25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ magnetic bio-MOF nanocomposites, and so the lesion growth was hindered. By using $25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ magnetic bio-MOFs, IFN- $\cdot\gamma$ production significantly increased while a comparatively low and unremarkable increase was observed for the level of IL-4. It can thus be inferred that magnetic bio-MOFs are capable of inducing an immune response from for the macrophage cells. Also, when the nanomaterial was introduced  into the mice, an increase was achieved in the defensive function of the macrophage cells. This finding agrees with previous results, which suggest that phagocytosis and cytokine-releasing functions of macrophages are enhanced in the presence of nanomaterials [70].
Although macrophage cells can be activated through the application of pro-inflammatory signaling, it may mediate the macrophage cells toxicity using magnetic bio-MOFs. As recorded in the literature, nanomaterial size is a determining factor in the clearance of particles by the macrophage cells and since phagocytosis and lavage in macrophage cells cannot affect the nano-sized particles, stress activation and ROS-mediated redox-sensitive transcription will be promoted through the pro-inflammatory TNF- $\mathfrak{a}$ signaling. These are therapeutically referred to as “macrophage self-destruction” characteristics and function as nanocarriers in macrophage-related disorders, atherosclerosis, bacterial infection, neuroinflammation, and rheumatoid arthritis.
According to the literature, when temperature rises over $39^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , it is impossible for leishmania parasites to increase in the macrophage cells [2, 3]. This result is of clinical importance and can be exploited for thermotherapy via light, heat bath, and laser therapy.  Obviously, CL treatment can effectively be conducted using thermotherapy. Besides, as it is known, CL treatment can be improved when local restricted heating is used. A kind of thermotherapy which might be considered for cancer therapy is hyperthermia, in which local heat in the injured tissues is increased. As heat is applied, an increase occurs in the tissue sensitivity towards radiation [1, 3]. Leishmaniasis treatment shows superior results when hyperthermia is used as a complimentary therepy. It can be suggested that provision of heat by the magnetic bio-MOFs might be due to the magnetization characteristics of the magnetic field parameters and specific formulations of MNP. As the results of the present study how, our prepared materials are highly promising for numerous biochemical uses.
## 4. Conclusion
Using ultrasound irradiation, the present study has prepared new and highly efficient magnetic bio-MOF (MBMOF) nanocomposites via a straightforward synthesis route. Various parameters, such as concentrations of initial reagents, synthesis duration, and irradiation frequency were optimized to obtain homogeneous morphology. The results show that non-aggregated particles with uniform, and  porous-layer open morphology can be obtained at $0.02{\bf\delta M}$ concentration with, 45 W at 120 min. Also, In order to show structure, surface area, crystallinity, magnetic properties, and chemical composition of the suggested nanocomposites, they have also been characterized. The results of characterization confirm that the desired, functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ bio-MOF nanocomposites are produced, possessing the appropriate magnetic characteristics, pureness of phase, and uniformity. For the first time, in the present work, effect of a bioMOF on $L.$ . major parasites under both in vitro and in vivo conditions has been taken into account. However, further studies are required to shed more light on the antileishmanial characteristics of the MBMOF nanocomposites. As the results show, using MBMOF nanocomposites, $L.$ . major promastigotes and amastigotes in mice can be suppressed. In the spleen cell cultures of the treated groups, a significant increase is observed in IFN-γ level. The cutaneous leishmaniasis lesions due to $L.$ . major in BALB/c mice decrease in the presence of the MBMOF nanocomposites. IC50 of MBMOFs has been calculated as $12.5\pm$ $0.47~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ after 72h. When the parasites are given the maximum dose of MBMOFs $(400~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ , the viability percentages of 0 and $29\%$ are obtained for $L$ . major promastigotes and macrophage cells, respectively. The MBMOF nanocomposites have shown considerable difference in their cytotoxic effects upon the promastigotes and amastigotes of $L$ . major $(\mathsf{P}{<}0.05)$ . Under in vivo conditions, a significant decrease has been observed in the sizes of lesions and load of parasites in the murine spleen and liver in test groups, in comparison with the control groups $(\mathrm{P}{<}0.05)$ . During the treatment, except in the mice group treated with MBMOF ointment $12.5~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ , the mice all lost body weight. Compared to the other test group and control groups, the mice given the higher dose $(25~\upmu\upmu\mathrm{{g}~}\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}})$ ), the highest level of IFN-γ has been observed $(\mathrm{P}{<}0.05)$ . Based on the results of in vitro and in vivo conditions, the MBMOF nanocomposites represent appropriate antileishmanial behaviors and can thus be used as a novel therapeutic ointment in the treatment of the cutaneous leishmaniasis.
